Q1
Rate-limit checks and statistics reads can interleave. Explain what in your implementation makes each decision and its counter updates atomic, so that every (client, endpoint) state and the aggregate statistics stay consistent; what this approach costs as traffic grows and how you would reduce that cost; and what would change if several server processes on the same machine enforced the same limits.

Every call to check() runs inside a single threading.Lock owned by the RateLimiter. Within that critical section the limiter reads the clock, prunes expired timestamps (Policy A) or refills the bucket (Policy B), makes the decision, writes the per-key state and increments total_allowed or total_rejected. stats() takes the same lock, so a reader can never see a decision without its counter update, or a key whose state is half-written. Reading the clock inside the lock matters too: if threads read the time first and then raced for the lock, a later timestamp could be appended before an earlier one and break the ordering the sliding-window deque relies on. The clock is time.monotonic(), sampled exactly once per decision. Unlike time.time(), it never jumps when NTP or an operator adjusts the wall clock, so elapsed-time arithmetic cannot go negative, expire a window early, over-refill a bucket or yield a negative reset_after_ms; and because pruning, the decision and the reset value all use that single sample, they describe the same instant. The policy methods assume the lock is already held and never re-acquire it, because threading.Lock is not re-entrant.

The provided aiohttp server calls check() from a single event-loop thread, so today the lock is uncontended. It becomes essential as soon as the limiter is used from a thread pool, run_in_executor or a threaded server, and because it is held for well under a microsecond it does not noticeably block the event loop.

The cost is serialization: every client and endpoint competes for one lock. Under the GIL this is cheap, but on free-threaded Python or under heavy traffic the lock becomes the throughput ceiling. I would reduce it with lock striping: hash each (client, endpoint) key to one of N shards, each with its own lock, state and counters, and have stats() sum the shards. Memory also grows with the number of keys, so idle keys should be evicted by a TTL sweep, and Policy A could switch to a sliding-window counter if storing up to `limit` timestamps per key becomes too expensive.

If several processes on the same machine enforced the same limits, each would keep its own dictionary, so a client could get limit x N requests through and the statistics would be split. threading.Lock does not cross process boundaries, so the state would have to move somewhere all processes share: a local Redis, a single limiter process that workers call over IPC, or shared memory guarded by a cross-process lock, the last being the most error-prone.


Q2
Explain how this architectural design would need to evolve for a multi-instance, horizontally scaled deployment (e.g., migrating from in-memory state to a distributed cache like Redis or Memcached).

Across instances behind a load balancer, in-memory state makes the effective limit scale with the number of instances, so the state must move to a shared store and each decision must stay atomic there. With Redis I would run each check as one Lua script (EVALSHA), which Redis executes atomically. Policy A keeps a sorted set per key: ZREMRANGEBYSCORE drops expired entries, ZCARD counts, ZADD records an accepted request, and the lowest score gives reset_after_ms. Policy B keeps a hash with tokens and last_refill and performs the same refill-then-decide arithmetic inside the script. The script should take the time from Redis TIME rather than from each instance, which removes clock skew between machines, and should set PEXPIRE on every key (the window for Policy A, about capacity / refill_rate for Policy B) so idle keys expire on their own. The global counters can be incremented in the same script or, if they become hot keys, aggregated per instance and flushed periodically.

Memcached has no server-side scripting, so the same logic would need gets/cas retry loops or would degrade to an approximate fixed-window INCR counter; Redis is the better fit. As traffic grows, Redis Cluster shards keys by hash slot, and because each check touches a single key the scripts stay single-slot.

The design also gains new failure modes. Every request now pays a network round trip, so calls need tight timeouts, and the gateway must choose between failing open (availability) and failing closed (protection) when Redis is unavailable, possibly falling back to the current in-memory limiter with per-instance limits. For very hot keys, instances can lease small batches of tokens from Redis to cut round trips at the cost of slight over-admission. A cheaper alternative is sticky routing with consistent hashing on client_id, which keeps this in-memory design, but state is lost whenever instances are added or removed and load can become uneven.
